VERDICT: FAIL
CHECKER: checker-second
FAMILY: adversarial
TASK: WS1
ATTEMPT: 3
---
METHOD
Built ./cmd/server from the snapshot (succeeds). Read the full WS1.3 diff
and manifest, re-derived R-FMT/R-EXACT/R-TEST from SPEC.md, ran the
existing suite (`go test ./internal/handlers/whatif/... ./internal/templates/...`
-> ok, ok) and the new JS test (`node --test whatif-quick-adjust.test.cjs`
-> 5/5 pass). Then did mutation-kill spot checks (revert one healthcare
display span to formatDollars -> TestWS1WholeDollarConvergence_HealthcareCard
fails; revert JS roundHalfEven to Math.round -> the JS convergence test
fails) confirming R-FMT's own tests are real, not tautological. All
mutation work done in throwaway `cp -a` copies under my scratch dir; the
given snapshot and .swarm/ files were never edited; no server was started
(binary built only, never run); no :8080/MCP contact; no git state changed.

R-FMT: PASS on everything I could construct. formatNumber (Go, half-even)
and formatWholeDollars/roundHalfEven (JS) agree at every .50 tie I tried,
including negative and cross-checked against a table matching Go's actual
%.0f output (verified with a standalone `go run` probe: 613.5->614,
612.5->612, 1234.5->1234, -0.5->-0, all half-even). grep confirms
formatDollars (half-up) is gone from every WS1-scoped stored-value
surface (healthcare card, Coverage Timeline, portfolio/living-expenses
slider+QA+phase-dollar label) — the pre-existing living-expenses split
named in the attempt-3 contract is fixed at all 4 sites. The OOB-swap
surface (`#healthcare-persons-list`, `#whatif-portfolio-settings-card`
etc. in whatif.html) reuses the SAME `whatif-healthcare-person` /
`whatif-portfolio-settings` template blocks as the initial render, so
served==live holds structurally, not just by test coincidence. Two
formatDollars call sites found still using half-up (Math.round-equivalent
formatAmount in whatif-rate-assumptions.js:159-160 for the per-account $
breakdown, and updateLivingExpensesPhaseNote's cents-precision phase
note) are BOTH byte-identical to base 643fa54 (`git show
643fa54:web/static/js/whatif-rate-assumptions.js` / `whatif-portfolio-
settings.js` diffed) and untouched by WS1.3's own diff — pre-existing,
out of scope per CLAUDE.md, reported as backlog observations only, not a
FAIL basis.

R-EXACT: FAIL, reproduced end-to-end. formatExact is a single function
(`strconv.FormatFloat(v, 'g', 15, 64)`) applied uniformly to every call
site, but R-EXACT's text specifies TWO different rules: a raw stored
value must use `strconv.FormatFloat(v,'f',-1,64)` ("never re-rounded"),
and only a "derived or scaled value" gets the 15-significant-digit cap.
The implementation's own doc comment admits the collapse ("Applies
uniformly at every exact-render call site, scaled or not: one helper, one
rule") and reasons only about MAGNITUDE ("up to $1e9"), never about
PRECISION/DEPTH — the two are different axes. A raw stored value whose
true shortest round-trip needs 16-17 significant digits (a $232,777
TaxableCostBasis produced by summing four cost-basis lots, e.g.
45123.17+98765.43+12345.678901234+76543.210987654 = 232777.48988888797,
a completely ordinary aggregation for a field the model's own comment
calls "the total cost basis" of a taxable brokerage, and a value a user
could equally type/paste directly since the field has no format
constraint beyond step="any") gets rendered by formatExact as
"232777.489888888" (15 sig figs) instead of the true "232777.48988888797"
(17 sig figs). I proved this is not merely a pure-function curiosity: I
rendered the REAL "whatif-rate-assumptions" template with this value,
extracted the actual `value=""` string the browser would see, submitted
it through the REAL `handleWhatIfSettings` handler exactly as an untouched
form would, and reloaded the saved settings:
  rendered/submitted value: "232777.489888888"
  loaded.TaxableCostBasis before: 232777.48988888797
  loaded.TaxableCostBasis after:  232777.489888888  (changed, diff=2.9e-11)
This is a real, reproducible violation of R-EXACT's literal text AND of
WS1's foundational Acceptance Criterion 1 ("submitting any /whatif form
... leaves every stored value unchanged (float-equal)") and D1's core
rule ("an untouched input never changes the saved value") — the exact bug
class the whole task exists to eliminate, via a different mechanism than
the original step-grid snap trap. None of the worker's own tests (generic
or hand-written) ever use a fixture value needing >15 significant digits
— every fixture is a "nice" 2-3 decimal number (276146.86, 388354.59,
etc.), so this gap is invisible to the existing suite and to the lead's
oracle (whose own fixture is likewise all clean decimals) — my attack
brief named this scenario almost verbatim ("does re-rounding a 16-17-
significant-digit stored value break 'never re-rounded'... (R-EXACT)?"),
so this is not a stretch reading of scope.

Secondary, lower-severity R-EXACT finding: formatExact also emits
scientific notation for |v| >= 1e15 or 0 < |v| < 1e-4 (e.g.
formatExact(1e15) = "1e+15", formatExact(0.00005) = "5e-05"), which
contradicts R-EXACT's unconditional "with no exponent". Verified in real
Chromium that `<input type=number>` DOES accept and correctly
round-trip such exponent-form values (`.value`/`.checkValidity()`/
`.valueAsNumber` all correct), so this does NOT break criteria 1/2
functionally, and it is not reachable from any of the ~30 WS1 fields'
realistic domains (portfolio/cost-basis cap near $1e9; percentages 0-100)
without an unusual hand-typed input. Reporting as a secondary observation
under the same root cause (the 'g',15 uniform rule), not an independent
FAIL basis on its own, but it reinforces that the raw-vs-scaled split
R-EXACT specifies was never implemented.

R-TEST: the required mutations are killed (spot-checked two: healthcare
display-span half-up revert, JS Math.round revert — both caught). The
suite does not, however, include any fixture exercising R-EXACT's
16-17-sig-fig raw-value case, which is exactly how the above defect
survived to this attempt.

CONCLUSION: R-FMT is solid and closes the attempt-2 hard-stop class
cleanly. R-EXACT is only half-implemented: the "raw value, never
re-rounded" half is not what the code does, and I demonstrated a live
save-corruption on an untouched submit as a direct consequence. This is
in-scope (formatExact is the worker's own new function, introduced by
this attempt, in a file already in the manifest) and squarely inside the
attempt-3 contract's own words. Per CLAUDE.md, default to FAIL on this
kind of demonstrated, in-scope, criterion-1-violating defect.

SCOPE
- FAIL basis: internal/templates/render.go's formatExact — in manifest,
  introduced/rewritten by this attempt.
- Backlog observations (NOT part of the FAIL): whatif-rate-assumptions.js
  per-account $ breakdown (formatAmount) and updateLivingExpensesPhaseNote
  cents display, both byte-identical to base and untouched by any WS1
  attempt.
